dep: atom memo in the C parser glue, C parser for use_reduce() calls with is_valid_flag

Sub-series: C (use_reduce parser) - patch 2 of 3; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.
Ported to upstream 73646c35d: unchanged (line numbers only)

_c_atom_from_c() built an Atom from a C parser token and then interned it;
about nine of ten tokens had been seen before (the implementers' count).
The interned, unevaluated Atom is now remembered per (token string, EAPI),
and USE conditionals are evaluated from the remembered instance.

use_reduce() left every call with an is_valid_flag callable to the Python
parser. The Python parser consults is_valid_flag only for the flags of
"flag?" groups and of USE conditionals inside atoms, so when the C parser
accepts the string and all those flags are valid, the C parser's result for
the same mode is the Python parser's result. _use_reduce_c_validated()
serves these calls behind the existing LRU cache (which therefore sees the
same keys in the same order), remembers per (string, EAPI) the tokens and
the flags to check, and returns None so that the Python parser runs and
decides whenever anything is unusual.

On top of patches 0001-0017, resolve seconds before -> after
(median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 30.0 -> 28.0, holds 15.1 -> 13.9, masks 32.0 -> 30.7,
  py313 18.4 -> 16.9, bdeps 21.9 -> 20.1, policykit 15.3 -> 14.3
  sum of six 132.7 -> 123.9 (-8.8 s, -2.0 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Patches 0001-0018 together: 447.5 -> 123.9 s (-72 %); 447.5 is the
stock sum of the ladder run of 2026-11-20, stock was not run again here.

Two pieces of the C parser glue grouped: the atom memo and the validated
use_reduce path; they were not measured apart in the chain.

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* The atom memo keeps every interned Atom alive for the life of the process.
* Equivalence with the Python parser is an argument plus the implementers'
  fuzzing (not repeated for this series).

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21. Not a human-written
change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on six states of
one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the same merge list
in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts and the same
package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
